🗓️ WEEK 1 — REQUIREMENT ANALYSIS AND PROBLEM STUDY

Date: Monday, 10 August 2026

🎯 Objective

The objective of Week 1 was to understand the billing problem in a multi-showroom mall, collect the requirements of customers and cashiers, and decide what FlashCart should do.

🛠️ Work Completed
Visited a mall and observed billing at three different showrooms.
Bought a shirt, earbuds and a coffee, and stood in three separate queues.
Collected three separate GST bills and compared how each one was calculated.
Listed the needs of the two users:
Customer
Cashier
Wrote the problem statement: one bag and one bill for the whole mall.
Listed the functional requirements:
Sign in with a mobile number
Scan product barcodes
One bag for all showrooms
One payment (UPI, card or cash)
One GST invoice
Cash desk with change calculation
Chose a web app instead of an Android app so that customers do not have to install anything.

⚠️ Error / Problem Faced

We could not agree on how one bill could carry different GST rates.

Clothes, electronics and food are taxed at different rates, but our first idea applied a single GST percentage to the whole bill.

✅ Resolution
Studied how GST works for retail goods.
Learnt that the GST rate belongs to each product, through its HSN code.
Decided that every product will store its own GST rate.
Decided that the bill will add up tax separately for each rate (5%, 12%, 18%).
Decided that the tax for each rate will be split into CGST and SGST.

🖼️ Project Picture

Three separate showroom bills from one mall visit, each with its own queue, payment and GST calculation — the problem FlashCart solves.

💡 Learning

We learned that getting the business rules right at the start saves a redesign later.

We also learned that requirements must come from real users and real situations, not only from our own assumptions.

